1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,850
Hello once again, hackers.

2
00:00:01,910 --> 00:00:02,920
Today we are pwning a

3
00:00:02,980 --> 00:00:05,830
program with the humble print function.

4
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:07,750
print f looks innocent:

5
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:09,160
compile a tiny C file,

6
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:10,670
run it, and hello world

7
00:00:10,730 --> 00:00:12,780
comes out. But that output

8
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:14,710
did not happen by magic.

9
00:00:14,770 --> 00:00:16,467
Something interpreted a recipe.

10
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:18,140
Every language has a way

11
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:19,910
to make numbers human:

12
00:00:19,970 --> 00:00:25,270
print, console.log, System.out.println, fmt.Println, println!, puts.

13
00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:27,130
Our target is C's print

14
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:30,270
f, the formatted output function.

15
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:32,160
The word formatted is doing

16
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:33,585
almost all the work here.

17
00:00:33,770 --> 00:00:34,900
The man page gives us

18
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:36,370
the whole crime scene in

19
00:00:36,430 --> 00:00:39,950
one line: printf(const char *format, ...).

20
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:42,150
One argument is the recipe.

21
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:44,090
The dots are however many

22
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:47,150
extra values that recipe asks for.

23
00:00:47,210 --> 00:00:49,550
Normal letters simply pass through.

24
00:00:49,610 --> 00:00:51,176
A percent sign makes print

25
00:00:51,236 --> 00:00:53,220
f stop being a typewriter

26
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:55,046
and become an interpreter.

27
00:00:55,210 --> 00:00:57,590
Percent d prints a decimal.

28
00:00:57,650 --> 00:00:59,870
Percent x prints hexadecimal.

29
00:00:59,930 --> 00:01:02,510
Percent p prints an address-shaped value.

30
00:01:02,570 --> 00:01:04,370
Percent s follows a pointer

31
00:01:04,430 --> 00:01:05,560
and reads bytes until a

32
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:09,450
null byte. Percent c prints

33
00:01:09,510 --> 00:01:11,470
one character, double percent prints

34
00:01:11,530 --> 00:01:12,940
a literal percent, and widths

35
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,831
such as percent zero eight

36
00:01:14,891 --> 00:01:16,510
x change padding.

37
00:01:16,570 --> 00:01:18,106
Percent twenty c can add

38
00:01:18,166 --> 00:01:20,330
twenty printed characters while showing

39
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:21,799
only one visible letter.

40
00:01:21,859 --> 00:01:23,700
Then percent n does something different:

41
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:25,370
it writes the printed-character count

42
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:26,667
through a pointer.

43
00:01:26,750 --> 00:01:29,686
That first argument is special.

44
00:01:29,746 --> 00:01:33,020
printf("percent s", input) keeps input

45
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:36,080
as data; even the string

46
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:38,128
percent x percent x percent

47
00:01:38,188 --> 00:01:41,110
x is printed literally.

48
00:01:41,170 --> 00:01:43,730
But printf(input) promotes the same

49
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:45,910
user bytes into the recipe.

50
00:01:45,970 --> 00:01:47,240
The wrong person is now

51
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:49,072
writing the recipe, so the

52
00:01:49,132 --> 00:01:50,990
interpreter starts asking for arguments

53
00:01:51,050 --> 00:01:52,976
the programmer never supplied.

54
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:55,530
Our challenge starts with a

55
00:01:55,590 --> 00:01:58,320
global variable named suss, equal

56
00:01:58,380 --> 00:01:59,887
to zero x two one

57
00:01:59,947 --> 00:02:01,910
seven three seven five seven three.

58
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:03,800
It reads a word into

59
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:05,960
buff, prints a fixed label

60
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:08,768
safely, and then calls print

61
00:02:08,828 --> 00:02:11,310
f, buff. Finally it opens

62
00:02:11,370 --> 00:02:13,764
the wizard gate only if

63
00:02:13,824 --> 00:02:15,935
suss equals zero x six

64
00:02:15,995 --> 00:02:17,822
seven six one six c

65
00:02:17,882 --> 00:02:19,530
six six. We do not

66
00:02:19,590 --> 00:02:21,830
need to smash the whole program.

67
00:02:21,890 --> 00:02:23,690
We only need to change

68
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:25,340
exactly this variable into exactly

69
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:26,242
that value.

70
00:02:26,450 --> 00:02:28,590
The first number is not random.

71
00:02:28,650 --> 00:02:31,341
In memory its bytes are

72
00:02:31,401 --> 00:02:33,230
seventy-three, seventy-five, seventy-three, twenty-one:

73
00:02:33,290 --> 00:02:35,190
s u s !.

74
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:37,420
The target integer looks like

75
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:38,180
g a l f when

76
00:02:38,230 --> 00:02:39,680
read left to right, but

77
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:41,750
this is little endian.

78
00:02:41,810 --> 00:02:45,040
In memory it becomes sixty-six,

79
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:46,640
six-c, sixty-one, sixty-seven:

80
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:47,750
f l a g.

81
00:02:47,810 --> 00:02:49,880
Our question is now mechanical:

82
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:51,570
how can typed text write

83
00:02:51,630 --> 00:02:53,057
these bytes into suss?

84
00:02:53,410 --> 00:02:55,300
First we run normal input.

85
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:57,300
hello prints as hello, and

86
00:02:57,360 --> 00:02:59,429
sus stays unchanged.

87
00:02:59,489 --> 00:03:01,804
Then we probe with percent

88
00:03:01,864 --> 00:03:04,120
p.percent p.percent p.percent p.

89
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:05,900
A safe program would print

90
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:08,510
those percent signs literally.

91
00:03:08,570 --> 00:03:11,870
This target prints pointer-looking values instead.

92
00:03:11,930 --> 00:03:14,710
That confirms a format string vulnerability:

93
00:03:14,770 --> 00:03:16,356
the format recipe is walking

94
00:03:16,416 --> 00:03:18,010
an argument shelf that was

95
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:19,999
never intended to be exposed.

96
00:03:20,170 --> 00:03:22,040
The actual call is just

97
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:23,090
print f, buff.

98
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:25,250
But after reading our percent

99
00:03:25,310 --> 00:03:27,630
p tokens, print f behaves

100
00:03:27,690 --> 00:03:30,207
as if it saw printf("percent

101
00:03:30,267 --> 00:03:35,710
p.percent p.percent p", arg1, arg2, arg3).

102
00:03:35,770 --> 00:03:37,380
It is not evil and

103
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:39,350
it is not random.

104
00:03:39,410 --> 00:03:41,590
The recipe requests ingredients;

105
00:03:41,650 --> 00:03:42,840
the chef was never given

106
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:44,740
them, so the machine reads

107
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:46,780
from the places where arguments

108
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:48,267
normally live. Percent p prints

109
00:03:48,327 --> 00:03:49,978
the value. Percent s follows it.

110
00:03:50,038 --> 00:03:53,138
Percent n writes through it.

111
00:03:53,490 --> 00:03:54,760
For control, we find where

112
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:57,070
our input lands in that shelf.

113
00:03:57,130 --> 00:03:58,990
We place A B C

114
00:03:59,050 --> 00:04:00,090
D E F G H

115
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:02,790
and try slots.

116
00:04:02,850 --> 00:04:04,900
At slot fourteen, the target

117
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:06,896
returns zero x four eight

118
00:04:06,956 --> 00:04:08,485
four seven four six four

119
00:04:08,545 --> 00:04:10,073
five four four four three

120
00:04:10,133 --> 00:04:11,345
four two four one.

121
00:04:11,405 --> 00:04:13,920
Flip that little-endian number into

122
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:15,620
memory order and it spells

123
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:16,750
A through H.

124
00:04:16,810 --> 00:04:17,920
So, in this local run,

125
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:19,770
argument fourteen is the first

126
00:04:19,830 --> 00:04:21,790
controlled eight-byte block.

127
00:04:21,850 --> 00:04:24,190
Alignment padding is not the exploit;

128
00:04:24,250 --> 00:04:26,110
it makes the recipe read

129
00:04:26,170 --> 00:04:27,837
exactly one clean eight-byte address.

130
00:04:28,510 --> 00:04:30,270
suss is global, so this

131
00:04:30,330 --> 00:04:33,340
local non-PIE target exposes it

132
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:35,290
in .data at zero x

133
00:04:35,350 --> 00:04:37,390
four zero four zero six zero.

134
00:04:37,450 --> 00:04:39,500
Two bytes higher is zero

135
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:41,342
x four zero four zero

136
00:04:41,402 --> 00:04:43,240
six two. We will write

137
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:45,061
the high half, zero x

138
00:04:45,121 --> 00:04:46,420
six seven six one, to

139
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:48,270
sus + 2, then the

140
00:04:48,330 --> 00:04:49,819
low half, zero x six

141
00:04:49,879 --> 00:04:57,190
c six six, to suss.

142
00:04:57,250 --> 00:04:58,440
After the first write the

143
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:00,510
bytes begin to look like flag;

144
00:05:00,570 --> 00:05:02,430
after the second, they are

145
00:05:02,490 --> 00:05:02,829
exactly flag.

146
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:04,800
Percent n does not print

147
00:05:04,860 --> 00:05:06,030
the letter n.

148
00:05:06,090 --> 00:05:07,280
It takes a pointer and

149
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:09,280
writes the number of characters

150
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:11,161
printed so far.

151
00:05:11,221 --> 00:05:14,180
printf("hellopercent n", &count) leaves count

152
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:15,630
equal to five.

153
00:05:15,690 --> 00:05:18,220
The h modifier makes percent

154
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:19,660
h n copy only the

155
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:21,250
low two bytes.

156
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:23,140
If our fake argument shelf

157
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:24,500
contains the address of sus,

158
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:26,220
the same feature becomes our

159
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:28,466
controlled two-byte write.

160
00:05:28,810 --> 00:05:30,610
One giant write would require

161
00:05:30,670 --> 00:05:31,530
more than one point seven

162
00:05:31,590 --> 00:05:33,470
billion printed characters.

163
00:05:33,530 --> 00:05:34,990
That is ridiculous.

164
00:05:35,050 --> 00:05:37,723
Instead, split the target:

165
00:05:37,783 --> 00:05:39,170
zero x six seven six

166
00:05:39,230 --> 00:05:41,091
one is twenty-six thousand four

167
00:05:41,151 --> 00:05:43,030
hundred sixty-five, and zero x

168
00:05:43,090 --> 00:05:45,113
six c six six is

169
00:05:45,173 --> 00:05:47,070
twenty-seven thousand seven hundred fifty.

170
00:05:47,130 --> 00:05:48,990
The first count is smaller,

171
00:05:49,050 --> 00:05:50,524
and the second is only

172
00:05:50,584 --> 00:05:51,460
one thousand two hundred eighty-five

173
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:53,350
more, so the counter moves

174
00:05:53,410 --> 00:05:54,733
naturally upward.

175
00:05:54,910 --> 00:06:01,610
The recipe is percent 14$26465cpercent

176
00:06:01,670 --> 00:06:05,890
19$hnpercent 14$1285cpercent 20$hn.

177
00:06:05,950 --> 00:06:08,060
First, a width field pumps

178
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:09,889
the count to zero x

179
00:06:09,949 --> 00:06:11,717
six seven six one.

180
00:06:11,777 --> 00:06:14,536
Slot nineteen contains packed sus

181
00:06:14,596 --> 00:06:16,155
+ 2, so percent h

182
00:06:16,215 --> 00:06:16,915
n writes the high half.

183
00:06:16,951 --> 00:06:17,651
Then another width adds zero

184
00:06:17,687 --> 00:06:18,387
x zero five zero five.

185
00:06:18,423 --> 00:06:20,580
Slot twenty contains packed suss,

186
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:25,404
so the second percent h

187
00:06:25,464 --> 00:06:28,860
n writes the low half.

188
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:30,185
The bytes after the recipe

189
00:06:30,245 --> 00:06:30,981
are not text for humans.

190
00:06:31,041 --> 00:06:31,777
They are address blocks placed

191
00:06:31,837 --> 00:06:32,466
exactly where printf will misread

192
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:32,466
them as arguments.

193
00:06:32,930 --> 00:06:34,490
During the final run, do

194
00:06:34,550 --> 00:06:37,230
not imagine twenty-six thousand literal

195
00:06:37,290 --> 00:06:38,830
spaces on screen.

196
00:06:38,890 --> 00:06:40,830
Imagine a counter ribbon.

197
00:06:40,890 --> 00:06:42,543
It reaches zero x six

198
00:06:42,603 --> 00:06:43,970
seven six one, writes at

199
00:06:44,030 --> 00:06:45,684
sus + 2, grows by

200
00:06:45,744 --> 00:06:46,800
one thousand two hundred eighty-five,

201
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:48,743
and writes zero x six

202
00:06:48,803 --> 00:06:50,270
c six six at sus.

203
00:06:50,330 --> 00:06:52,510
The ordinary program then checks

204
00:06:52,570 --> 00:06:54,052
its variable, finds zero x

205
00:06:54,112 --> 00:06:54,885
six seven six one six

206
00:06:54,945 --> 00:06:56,250
c six six, and opens

207
00:06:56,310 --> 00:06:57,870
its own win branch.

208
00:06:57,930 --> 00:06:58,810
The flag text here is

209
00:06:58,870 --> 00:06:59,570
a demo placeholder, not a

210
00:06:58,870 --> 00:07:03,288
claimed live flag.

211
00:07:03,530 --> 00:07:05,390
The payload looks like wizard

212
00:07:05,450 --> 00:07:07,750
language, but the mechanism is boring.

213
00:07:07,810 --> 00:07:09,880
One: user input controls the

214
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:12,070
first printf argument.

215
00:07:12,130 --> 00:07:15,630
Two: format tokens consume argument slots.

216
00:07:15,690 --> 00:07:18,170
Three: our own bytes appear

217
00:07:18,230 --> 00:07:20,030
in those slots.

218
00:07:20,090 --> 00:07:21,360
Four: we place the address

219
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:23,110
of sus there.

220
00:07:23,170 --> 00:07:24,980
Five: percent h n writes

221
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:28,007
the counter through that address.

222
00:07:28,067 --> 00:07:30,609
Recipes, slots, addresses, counts, writes.

223
00:07:30,830 --> 00:07:32,270
The fix is almost insulting

224
00:07:32,330 --> 00:07:34,670
in how small it is:

225
00:07:34,730 --> 00:07:36,620
never let untrusted input become

226
00:07:36,680 --> 00:07:39,510
the format. Replace print f,

227
00:07:39,570 --> 00:07:41,690
buff with print f, percent

228
00:07:41,750 --> 00:07:43,790
s, buff. Feed it the

229
00:07:43,850 --> 00:07:45,190
same scary input;

230
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:47,120
the machine reads one fixed

231
00:07:47,180 --> 00:07:49,270
percent s socket, treats the

232
00:07:49,330 --> 00:07:51,350
full string as green data,

233
00:07:51,410 --> 00:07:53,790
prints it literally, and leaves

234
00:07:53,850 --> 00:07:54,810
sus unchanged.

235
00:07:55,170 --> 00:07:57,700
When solving these, sort specifiers

236
00:07:57,760 --> 00:07:59,390
by what they give you.

237
00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:01,320
Percent p and percent x

238
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:04,260
observe values. Percent s follows

239
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:05,560
a pointer to read a

240
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:07,950
string, if the address is valid.

241
00:08:08,010 --> 00:08:10,450
Percent n, percent h n,

242
00:08:10,510 --> 00:08:14,110
and percent h h n

243
00:08:14,170 --> 00:08:17,249
write state. Leaks help us locate.

244
00:08:17,309 --> 00:08:19,517
Writes change what the program does.

245
00:08:20,170 --> 00:08:21,450
Percent n does not write

246
00:08:21,510 --> 00:08:22,930
a number typed inside the

247
00:08:22,990 --> 00:08:25,180
format string. It copies the

248
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:28,550
number of characters printed so far.

249
00:08:28,610 --> 00:08:30,072
That is why the large

250
00:08:30,132 --> 00:08:33,630
widths exist: they are counter fuel.

251
00:08:33,690 --> 00:08:36,350
Keyboard bytes become a printf recipe;

252
00:08:36,410 --> 00:08:38,230
the recipe creates output;

253
00:08:38,290 --> 00:08:40,470
the output creates a counter;

254
00:08:40,530 --> 00:08:42,390
then percent h n copies

255
00:08:42,450 --> 00:08:44,224
the counter into memory.

256
00:08:44,610 --> 00:08:47,040
The dollar syntax makes this precise.

257
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:49,540
Non-positional tokens walk one shelf

258
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:51,190
slot after another.

259
00:08:51,250 --> 00:08:54,210
percent 19$hn jumps directly to

260
00:08:54,270 --> 00:08:57,530
slot nineteen; percent 20$hn jumps

261
00:08:57,590 --> 00:08:58,950
to slot twenty.

262
00:08:59,010 --> 00:09:02,420
And because scanf("percent 1024s", buf)

263
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:04,650
stops at whitespace, we do

264
00:09:04,710 --> 00:09:07,156
not type literal spaces.

265
00:09:07,216 --> 00:09:09,560
Width specifiers make printf create

266
00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:10,236
them for us.

267
00:09:10,570 --> 00:09:12,390
print f did not get hacked.

268
00:09:12,450 --> 00:09:13,720
It followed the recipe it

269
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:15,270
was given. The bug was

270
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:17,670
letting the user write that recipe.

271
00:09:17,730 --> 00:09:20,960
Safe code makes data become output.

272
00:09:21,020 --> 00:09:22,210
Unsafe code lets data become

273
00:09:22,270 --> 00:09:25,530
a recipe, interpretation become memory

274
00:09:25,590 --> 00:09:27,510
access, and with percent n,

275
00:09:27,570 --> 00:09:30,870
memory access become memory modification.

276
00:09:30,930 --> 00:09:33,030
Fix it with print f,

277
00:09:33,090 --> 00:09:34,910
percent s, buff.

278
00:09:34,970 --> 00:09:36,000
See you in the next

279
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:36,713
one, hackers.

280
00:09:37,130 --> 00:09:38,930
Before we leave, replay the

281
00:09:38,990 --> 00:09:40,050
exploit as a checklist you

282
00:09:40,110 --> 00:09:41,100
could use on the next

283
00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:43,150
format string challenge.

284
00:09:43,210 --> 00:09:44,400
First, read the source and

285
00:09:44,460 --> 00:09:45,670
find the call site.

286
00:09:45,730 --> 00:09:47,530
The dangerous shape is not

287
00:09:47,590 --> 00:09:48,650
merely a percent sign in

288
00:09:48,710 --> 00:09:50,090
user input. It is user

289
00:09:50,150 --> 00:09:52,050
input becoming the first argument

290
00:09:52,110 --> 00:09:53,860
to printf. Second, run one

291
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:56,530
harmless probe. A sequence such

292
00:09:56,590 --> 00:09:58,560
as percent p, dot, percent

293
00:09:58,620 --> 00:10:00,960
p tells you whether the

294
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:02,200
program is interpreting your input

295
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:03,270
or printing it literally.

296
00:10:03,330 --> 00:10:05,270
Third, put an eight-byte marker

297
00:10:05,330 --> 00:10:06,205
in the input and scan

298
00:10:06,265 --> 00:10:07,950
the positional slots.

299
00:10:08,010 --> 00:10:09,100
When the marker comes back

300
00:10:09,160 --> 00:10:10,220
as a hexadecimal value, you

301
00:10:10,280 --> 00:10:10,980
know where your own bytes

302
00:10:10,280 --> 00:10:13,481
sit from printf's point of view.

303
00:10:13,660 --> 00:10:16,430
Fourth, identify the state you

304
00:10:16,490 --> 00:10:17,270
actually need to change.

305
00:10:17,330 --> 00:10:18,600
In this target, that state

306
00:10:18,660 --> 00:10:20,848
is the global variable sus.

307
00:10:20,908 --> 00:10:23,080
The winning comparison asks for

308
00:10:23,140 --> 00:10:24,820
one precise integer, and the

309
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:26,630
byte view reveals that the

310
00:10:26,690 --> 00:10:28,990
value spells flag in memory.

311
00:10:29,050 --> 00:10:30,920
Fifth, remember that percent n

312
00:10:30,980 --> 00:10:32,660
does not magically write a

313
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:34,750
number typed in the payload.

314
00:10:34,810 --> 00:10:36,000
It copies the number of

315
00:10:36,060 --> 00:10:38,120
output characters printed so far

316
00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:39,644
through a pointer.

317
00:10:39,704 --> 00:10:41,480
Width specifiers are how we

318
00:10:41,540 --> 00:10:44,382
steer that counter.

319
00:10:44,442 --> 00:10:47,855
Positional specifiers are how we

320
00:10:47,915 --> 00:10:48,910
select the address.

321
00:10:48,970 --> 00:10:49,763
Packed bytes in the tail

322
00:10:49,823 --> 00:10:50,523
of the input are how

323
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:51,020
we place that address in

324
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:51,384
a slot.

325
00:10:51,730 --> 00:10:53,264
For the write itself, choose

326
00:10:53,324 --> 00:10:54,440
a size that makes the

327
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:57,070
arithmetic boring. Two halfword writes

328
00:10:57,130 --> 00:10:58,560
are easier to reason about

329
00:10:58,620 --> 00:11:00,256
than one giant integer.

330
00:11:00,316 --> 00:11:01,520
Write the smaller half first,

331
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:03,440
then add only the difference

332
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:05,110
before the second write.

333
00:11:05,170 --> 00:11:06,980
Watch the byte row after

334
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:09,414
every operation. If the little-endian

335
00:11:09,474 --> 00:11:10,475
bytes say f, l, a,

336
00:11:10,535 --> 00:11:12,740
g, the integer comparison will

337
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:15,248
see the value the branch expects.

338
00:11:15,650 --> 00:11:17,840
That method is useful because

339
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:21,270
it replaces mystery with observations:

340
00:11:21,330 --> 00:11:23,200
inspect the recipe, leak the

341
00:11:23,260 --> 00:11:25,330
shelf, find the marker, locate

342
00:11:25,390 --> 00:11:27,700
the target, count carefully, and

343
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:30,060
write only what the challenge

344
00:11:30,120 --> 00:11:32,130
asks for. And the defensive

345
00:11:32,190 --> 00:11:34,150
lesson remains tiny.

346
00:11:34,210 --> 00:11:37,300
Keep the format string fixed.

347
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:40,390
Pass untrusted input as data.

348
00:11:40,450 --> 00:11:42,320
When the program owns the

349
00:11:42,380 --> 00:11:44,930
recipe, printf is just output again.
